WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.260
Every handler has a boundary, and every boundary needs validation.

2
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:14.840
HTTP requests need expected methods, paths, headers, bodies, sizes, types, formats, and authentication context.

3
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:23.500
Queue messages, event bus messages, object metadata, webhook payloads, scheduled inputs, and database events need the same discipline.

4
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:35.840
A managed service may deliver the event, but the payload can still contain user-controlled fields, stale state, unexpected nesting, oversized values, unsafe paths, or forged workflow data.

5
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:47.880
Do not trust client-supplied user IDs, tenant IDs, roles, object keys, file paths, callback URLs, prices, quantities, or workflow state.

6
00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:52.460
Authentication proves who is calling or what system produced the event.

7
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.320
Authorization decides whether that caller or event is allowed to perform this action on this resource.

8
00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:04.660
For multi-tenant systems, object-level and tenant-aware authorization must be enforced server-side.

9
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:13.000
A function that processes storage events still needs to understand whether the object key and metadata are valid for the tenant and workflow.

10
00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.960
Webhooks and cross-service events deserve special care.

11
00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:25.380
Verify signatures, source identity, timestamps, replay windows, and idempotency keys where the design requires them.

12
00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:32.680
A webhook handler that accepts any JSON body from the internet is not protected simply because it runs behind an API gateway.

13
00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:38.940
A queue worker that trusts every message is not protected simply because the queue is managed.

14
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:41.920
Validation belongs at the boundary of each handler.

15
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.520
Event schemas make review easier.

16
00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:53.340
Define expected fields, types, sizes, ranges, formats, required values, and rejected values.

17
00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:59.840
Use validation libraries or shared schema code where they reduce mistakes, but keep the business rules visible.

18
00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:04.820
Validation supports security and reliability, but it does not replace authorization.

19
00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:12.920
A valid event can still be unauthorized, and an authenticated caller can still request a resource they do not own.
